 "result_text": "B902 now treats a class that directly subclasses `type` as a metaclass. I haven't run the tests or tried it on the `StorageMeta` example.\n\nIn the `bugbear.py` change (`check_for_b902`), a metaclass's instance methods, such as `__init__(cls, name, bases, d)`, now expect `cls` or `klass` as the first argument instead of `self`. Its `__new__` and classmethods expect `metacls`, `metaclass`, `mcs` or `mcls`. The error message labels these as \"metaclass instance\" and \"metaclass class\" methods.\n\nDetection only looks for a literal `type` in the class's bases. A metaclass that inherits from another metaclass (say `class B(StorageMeta)`) is still checked as an ordinary class, so its `__init__(cls, ...)` would still be flagged.\n\nI didn't add tests, as you asked.",
